      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Prophase initiates with chromatin condensing into visible chromosomes, while the mitotic spindle begins to form from centrosomes, a critical step to understand. During metaphase, the spindle fibers attach to the kinetochores at the centromeres, aligning all chromosomes precisely along the metaphase plate, which is essential for accurate division. Anaphase sees the sister chromatids being pulled apart by the shortening spindle fibers toward opposite poles, a mechanism students must learn. Telophase concludes the process as the chromosomes decondense, and new nuclear envelopes form around the two sets of genetic material, allowing the resulting cells to function properly.",
